---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="NPC names"
  description="The cast of characters and the rules for what we call them. Most names come from Magician's Quest 1's English localization."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Translation Canon', href: `${base}/field-guide/translation-canon/` },
    { label: 'NPC names' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Tongari Boushi reuses many characters from
    <em>Magician's Quest: Mysterious Times</em>, the studio's earlier
    DS game that had an English release. Wherever a character appears
    in both games, we use Konami's official Game 1 English name.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some of those names look <em>very</em> different from the
    Japanese. リカルド is not "Ricardo" — it's <strong>Mr. Vlad</strong>.
    ドルイドン is not "Druidon" — it's <strong>Mr. Oakley</strong>. We
    don't get to second-guess these. They're locked.
  </p>

  <h2>Key NPC names</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Japanese</th><th>Official English (Konami)</th><th>Role</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>リカルド</td><td>Mr. Vlad</td><td>Teacher / vampire character</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ドルイドン</td><td>Mr. Oakley</td><td>Teacher</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ミスキーキー</td><td>Miss Strawe</td><td>Teacher</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ダグラス</td><td>Mr. Graves</td><td>Teacher</td></tr>
      <tr><td>こうちょう</td><td>Principal Sol</td><td>The school principal</td></tr>
      <tr><td>テツ</td><td>Reginald</td><td>Recurring side character (from Game 1)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ヒゲマル</td><td>Charlie</td><td>Postman, recurring</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ビビ</td><td>Vivian</td><td>Shopkeeper (Vivian's Boutique)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>トメ</td><td>Malila</td><td>Side character</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ピカード</td><td>Piquard</td><td>Side character</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ラム</td><td>Felicia</td><td>Side character</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ミント</td><td>Mint</td><td>Side character</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ロック</td><td>Rock</td><td>Side character</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Honorifics</h2>
  <p>
    Teachers and other adult authority figures get "Mr." or "Miss"
    prefixes in English even though Japanese uses a bare name or
    "-sensei." This matches Game 1's convention. The principal is
    "Principal Sol," not "Mr. Sol" — also matching Game 1.
  </p>

  <h2>Where there's no Game 1 canon</h2>
  <p>
    Some NPCs are Tongari-specific — they don't appear in Magician's
    Quest 1. For those, we picked names from context, leaning toward
    spellings that match the rest of the cast in feel:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>NPCs whose Japanese name is a katakana rendering of a
      Western name (like ピカード → Piquard) get a Western spelling.</li>
    <li>NPCs with Japanese names (like テツ — though in this case
      Game 1's official name <em>Reginald</em> applies) get treated
      case-by-case.</li>
    <li>NPCs whose Japanese name is an adjective or noun
      ("ラム" = "rum") usually get a different Western-style name to
      avoid being awkward.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For these fresh translations we keep notes on why we chose a
    given form. If a better name surfaces later, the canonicalization
    pass will update every affected dialog row in one shot.
  </p>

  <h2>The pronoun and particle system</h2>
  <p>
    Each NPC has six "personality particle" variants (cheerful,
    timid, gruff, etc.) and one first-person pronoun. These are
    looked up at runtime via opcodes — see
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/opcodes/`}>the
    opcode menagerie</a>. The English side preserves the placeholder
    bytes for these substitutions. We don't translate the particle
    set directly; instead we make sure the English line reads
    naturally around the substitution.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
